<exercisegroup cols="2" xml:id="exercisegroup-vector-geom-unit-parallel-to-line">
<title>Creating unit vectors parallel to a line in <m> \R^2 </m></title>

<introduction><p>
	In each case,
	compute the two unit vectors that are parallel to the given line.
</p></introduction>

<exercise>

	<statement><p><m> \ell \colon \;\; x - 3 y = 4 </m></p></statement>

	<solution><p>
		This is the line from <xref ref="exercise-vectors-parallel-to-line-x-minus-3y" />,
		where it was determined that vector <m> \uvec{v} = (3,1) </m> is parallel to the line.
		Normalize and then negate that normalized vector to obtain parallel unit vectors
		<md><mrow>
			  \uvec{u} \amp= \left(   \frac{3}{\sqrt{10}},   \frac{1}{\sqrt{10}} \right) \text{,}
			\amp
			- \uvec{u} \amp= \left( - \frac{3}{\sqrt{10}}, - \frac{1}{\sqrt{10}} \right) \text{.}
		</mrow></md>
	</p></solution>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p><m>\ell \colon \;\; 2 x + 5 y = 5</m></p></statement>

	<solution><p>
		This is the line from <xref ref="exercise-vectors-parallel-to-line-2x-plus-5y" />,
		where it was determined that vector <m> \uvec{v} = (5,-2) </m> is parallel to the line.
		Normalize and then negate that normalized vector to obtain parallel unit vectors
		<md><mrow>
			  \uvec{u} \amp= \left(   \frac{5}{\sqrt{29}}, - \frac{2}{\sqrt{29}} \right) \text{,}
			  \amp
			- \uvec{u} \amp= \left( - \frac{5}{\sqrt{29}},   \frac{2}{\sqrt{29}} \right) \text{.}
		</mrow></md>
	</p></solution>

</exercise>

</exercisegroup>
